You have to pull that down and sync your changes

00:52:55.556 --> 00:52:57.995
so that your version of main

00:52:57.995 --> 00:53:01.436
now looks like what the one at the origin looks like,

00:53:01.436 --> 00:53:02.275
right?

00:53:03.635 --> 00:53:05.476
And your origin might live on a server too,

00:53:05.476 --> 00:53:08.535
it might not even live on GitHub server.

00:53:10.476 --> 00:53:13.476
So really, main is just another WorkTree,

00:53:13.476 --> 00:53:16.556
it's just like a version of your origin.

00:53:16.556 --> 00:53:18.116
So what Barrett is saying is,

00:53:18.116 --> 00:53:21.155
we don't ever go check out main,

00:53:21.155 --> 00:53:25.196
it's just a view of your origin.

00:53:25.196 --> 00:53:27.875
And so all of these are just views of the origin,

00:53:28.755 --> 00:53:30.275
but they're actual copies, because they're clones.

00:53:31.755 --> 00:53:36.436
Before WorkTrees, I actually saw Joe Chang

00:53:36.436 --> 00:53:40.515
have six or eight different copies of R Shiny

00:53:40.515 --> 00:53:42.155
on his computer.

00:53:42.155 --> 00:53:43.915
How big is that?

00:53:43.915 --> 00:53:45.556
Because he would just check out,

00:53:45.556 --> 00:53:47.875
he's like, oh, I don't wanna like deal with this right now.

00:53:47.875 --> 00:53:51.275
So he would just do a git clone into a new folder,

00:53:51.275 --> 00:53:53.995
and like, he would just manage it manually

00:53:53.995 --> 00:53:54.836
every once in a while.

00:53:54.836 --> 00:53:57.236
I don't know how he kept it straight in his head.

00:53:58.875 --> 00:54:02.716
But yeah, before WorkTrees, it was just the Wild West.

00:54:03.915 --> 00:54:08.076
So Tom asks, so main is only relevant when you merge,

00:54:08.076 --> 00:54:11.076
and it's a moving target otherwise?

00:54:11.076 --> 00:54:11.915
Yes.

00:54:11.915 --> 00:54:14.076
I think, yeah, I think that's a good description.

00:54:16.316 --> 00:54:19.675
Brent says, my brain broke in the first five minutes.

00:54:19.675 --> 00:54:20.716
I hope it didn't.

00:54:20.716 --> 00:54:22.836
I hope everybody is a little bit closer

00:54:22.836 --> 00:54:25.515
to understanding what a WorkTree is,

00:54:25.515 --> 00:54:27.675
the utility of WorkTrees,

00:54:28.356 --> 00:54:29.515
the use cases for WorkTrees,

00:54:29.515 --> 00:54:33.956
and maybe a little bit of how you might need them

00:54:33.956 --> 00:54:35.556
or utilize them.

00:54:35.556 --> 00:54:37.436
And I really recommend,

00:54:37.436 --> 00:54:39.836
and I was talking with Jenny Bryant about this too,

00:54:39.836 --> 00:54:41.356
the best way to play with these

00:54:41.356 --> 00:54:43.795
is just to go open something that doesn't matter

00:54:43.795 --> 00:54:44.635
and play with it.

00:54:44.635 --> 00:54:48.035
Create a WorkTree, do something with it, merge it,

00:54:48.035 --> 00:54:50.395
nonsensical changes, just open up text files,

00:54:50.395 --> 00:54:51.716
it doesn't matter.

00:54:51.716 --> 00:54:54.316
See how they work, look at the folder structure.

00:54:55.236 --> 00:54:57.915
When you open one on Positron, where does it put it?

00:54:57.915 --> 00:54:59.236
Right, like go look at that,

00:54:59.236 --> 00:55:01.596
go look at your folder structure

00:55:01.596 --> 00:55:04.436
because you might not have the same sort of like

00:55:04.436 --> 00:55:07.875
control panel that Barrett has here in Conductor

00:55:07.875 --> 00:55:09.236
if you don't use Conductor.

00:55:10.275 --> 00:55:11.236
Let's see.

00:55:11.236 --> 00:55:12.236
I wanted to add something

00:55:12.236 --> 00:55:13.836
because Garrick had just added in the chat

00:55:13.836 --> 00:55:15.476
and I know we have two minutes left.

00:55:15.476 --> 00:55:16.995
Garrick says, typically your WorkTrees

00:55:16.995 --> 00:55:18.316
are checked out to folders

00:55:18.316 --> 00:55:20.635
that are adjacent to your one true repo.

00:55:20.635 --> 00:55:24.035
So for example, I keep my local copy of ShinyChat

00:55:24.716 --> 00:55:27.716
in one repo that is like on the PositDev path

00:55:30.395 --> 00:55:31.236
to ShinyChat.

00:55:31.236 --> 00:55:32.795
And when you create a new WorkTree,

00:55:32.795 --> 00:55:35.836
you add a new folder with a whole copy of the repo

00:55:35.836 --> 00:55:37.675
somewhere else on your computer.

00:55:37.675 --> 00:55:39.755
I usually put these in folders

00:55:39.755 --> 00:55:43.155
with like main-repo.WorkTrees

00:55:43.155 --> 00:55:46.155
so that my ShinyChat WorkTrees end up in,

00:55:46.155 --> 00:55:48.795
you know, like ShinyChat.WorkTrees.

00:55:48.795 --> 00:55:50.795
I think that that makes a lot of sense.

00:55:51.795 --> 00:55:54.155
He says, I think Conductor kind of hides

00:55:54.155 --> 00:55:55.436
that one true repo view

00:55:55.436 --> 00:55:57.635
because it's managing all the WorkTrees for you.

00:55:57.635 --> 00:56:01.995
It's doing all of the Conductor work, which we like.

00:56:01.995 --> 00:56:06.596
So my origin one here is inside my, you know,

00:56:06.596 --> 00:56:09.076
RStudio Shiny React, Shiny React NoSync.

00:56:09.076 --> 00:56:10.556
Like that's my original.

00:56:10.556 --> 00:56:12.196
And then all of my WorkTrees

00:56:12.196 --> 00:56:15.755
are inside Barrett Conductor WorkSpaces Shiny React.

00:56:15.755 --> 00:56:17.875
So they're all over here, full copies.

00:56:18.875 --> 00:56:19.915
Yes.

00:56:19.915 --> 00:56:22.076
And then Nathan had asked,

00:56:22.076 --> 00:56:23.675
are we operating in a world

00:56:23.675 --> 00:56:26.236
where you don't push directly to main?

00:56:26.236 --> 00:56:27.995
You just make a new branch.

00:56:27.995 --> 00:56:31.155
You still do PR to main.

00:56:31.155 --> 00:56:36.155
You are PRing to main origin, which is up remotely.

00:56:36.875 --> 00:56:38.915
So that's a fair one.

00:56:38.915 --> 00:56:41.116
If you were to do work in main

00:56:41.116 --> 00:56:43.196
and then boss comes in and says,

00:56:43.196 --> 00:56:45.236
you need to fix this right now.

00:56:45.236 --> 00:56:48.316
You have to clean up that working area,

00:56:48.316 --> 00:56:49.795
that stove, that kitchen stove.

00:56:49.795 --> 00:56:54.795
You have to clean it up before making your fix.

00:56:55.076 --> 00:56:58.476
If you were on a working branch or a WorkTree,

00:56:58.476 --> 00:57:01.675
you could do that just freely.

00:57:01.675 --> 00:57:02.795
You would just make a new one,

00:57:02.795 --> 00:57:04.596
make the fix and merge back to main.

00:57:04.596 --> 00:57:06.875
So personally, make small PRs,

00:57:06.875 --> 00:57:09.476
make many, many, many, many PRs

00:57:09.476 --> 00:57:12.356
and merge often and quickly.

00:57:12.356 --> 00:57:15.716
And WorkTrees allow for that.

00:57:16.915 --> 00:57:17.755
Amazing.

